From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 02:02:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: render sample paths by their actual shape (dots, steps,
 or line)

make_sample_path() gains a style= parameter ("line" | "dots" | "steps")
so each kind of sample path is drawn the way it actually behaves,
instead of always as a plain connected line:

- Tuple.plot(), InfiniteVector.plot(), and DiscreteTimeFunction.plot()
  now default to style="dots" -- a marker at each index/time step
  joined by a dashed line -- since there is nothing "between" one
  discrete step and the next.
- ContinuousTimeFunction.plot() now detects a continuous-time,
  discrete-valued jump process (anything mixing in DiscreteValued --
  PoissonProcess, ContinuousTimeMarkovChain, renewal/compound-Poisson
  processes, queues, YuleProcess, ...) and draws it from its exact
  jump times and states (style="steps"), rather than sampling it on a
  fixed 200-point grid that only approximates where it actually jumps.
  A new _discrete_valued_step_path() helper walks the states/
  interarrival_times sequence documented on DiscreteValued to build
  the exact step data.
- Every other continuous-time function (a genuinely smooth path like
  Brownian motion) keeps today's dense-grid plain-line rendering,
  unchanged.
- A DiscreteValued path that only implements get_states() and not
  get_arrival_times() (the documented NonHomogeneousPoissonProcess/
  CoxProcess gap in CLAUDE.md) falls back to the dense-grid line
  instead of raising, so this stays a plotting-only change.

Regression tests added in test_plot.py (make_sample_path's style=
parameter directly) and test_result.py (each Result type's default
style, the exact step data at various tmin/tmax windows, the smooth-
path no-op case, and the AttributeError fallback).
